{
  "id": "sum-of-levels-in-binary-tree",
  "questionId": "sum-of-levels-in-binary-tree",
  "slug": "sum-of-levels-in-binary-tree",
  "title": "Sum of Levels in Binary Tree",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you need a running total per level - what if you processed the queue one level's worth of nodes at a time?",
    "snapshot the queue's current size before draining it - that count is exactly this level's node count",
    "add up the values of just those nodes into a running total for the level, then push their valid children for the next round",
    "append each level's total to the result before moving to the next round; stop when the queue empties"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "accidentally summing across all levels into one running total instead of resetting per level",
    "including sentinel or out-of-bounds children in the sum by not checking validity before enqueuing",
    "off-by-one in the level count when snapshotting the queue size after some nodes have already been added for the next level"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a\ncomplete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node here\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 can never be a genuine value; an index at or past the end of the array also counts as missing\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2 has no left child (sentinel) but does have a right child, 4.\n\n**Return an array where entry i is the sum of all node values at depth i, starting with the root's depth (0) first and going down to the deepest level last.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= tree.length <= 2000",
      "-100 <= tree[i] <= 100 for every real node value",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree",
      "negative values that make a level sum negative",
      "a level with only one real node while its siblings' subtree is entirely sentinel"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 9, 20, -101, -101, 15, 7]",
        "output": "[3, 29, 22]",
        "expectedOutput": "[3, 29, 22]",
        "explanation": "level 0 sums to 3, level 1 sums to 9+20=29, level 2 sums to 15+7=22"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1]",
        "output": "[1]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1]",
        "explanation": "one node, one level"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]",
        "output": "[1, 5, 22]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 5, 22]",
        "explanation": "level 0 sums to 1, level 1 sums to 2+3=5, level 2 sums to 4+5+6+7=22"
      }
    ],
